  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 217 AM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nOnce the potent Pacific storm exits the area to the east and \naccelerates into the Great Lakes Region, primary forecast concern \nwill shift to a hard freeze and frost Thursday night into Friday \nmorning as clouds dissipate and skies clear for the night. Becoming \nincreasing confident that a growing-season ending hard freeze is \ncertain for most of the area as even the 90th percentile (warm-side) \nNBM and Grand Ensemble lows are in the mid to upper 20s for a large \nbulk of the forecast area. Freeze headlines are expected soon with \nlow temperatures forecast to range from the mid teens to mid 20s, \nbut want to handle the storm system impacting the area today and \nThursday first. Otherwise, it will start to warm up on Friday as the \neastern Pacific ridge axis translates east into the Intermountain \nWest. Northwest flow will continue over the forecast area though, so \nhighs will be seasonably cool and generally in the 50s to low 60s \nacross the region. \n\nFor the weekend, medium to long Range models continue to be in good \nagreement, showing the broad upper level ridge axis translating \neastward into the Front Range. Expect drier weather with pleasant \ndaytime temperatures for mid October with highs in the 60s to low \n70s...and some mid 70s for western Nebraska. Low temperatures will \nbe seasonable and generally in the 30s to low 40s into next Monday \nmorning. Saturday morning will be the coldest morning with lows \ngenerally in the mid 20s to mid 30s with some frost possible. A weak \ncold front will brush by the area Saturday night, resulting in \ntemperatures a few degrees cooler than Saturday and gusty westerly \nwinds on Saturday shifting into the north on Sunday. \n\nFurther out, all models show the upper level ridge axis shifting \neast into the central and eastern portion of the United States as \nanother Pacific system moves near the west coast by late Monday and \nTuesday. Tuesday afternoon will likely be the warmest day of next \nweek with highs in the mid 60s to mid 70s due to south to southwest \nflow at the surface and aloft. Models show this storm system slowly \ntracking eastward over the western United States with increasing \nchances of light rain and cooler temperatures by next Wednesday and \nthrough the end of next week.",
